I'm joined today by Tyler van der wiel  who's a professor of Epidemiology at the  Harvard School of Public Health and  studies associations between religious  participation and health you actually  have a recent study on this issue and it  had some interesting correlations  between women's religious service  attendants and health outcomes get give  our audience sort of an overview of what  you found yeah so we published a few  studies in the last couple of months  which showed that women who regularly  attend religious services generally have  better health we found for example that  women who were attending religious  services more than once a week were  about thirty three percent less likely  to die in 16 years of follow-up than  those not attending at all those who  were attending weekly about twenty six  percent less likely to die and follow up  in those attending a less than weekly  maybe one to three times a month were  about thirteen percent less likely to  die during follow-up and we also looked  at em suicide and depression as as  outcomes and women who were regularly  attending services were about five times  less likely to commit suicide and about  thirty percent likely and less likely to  subsequently become depressed so they  were fairly a substantial associations  can you talk a little bit about with a  study like this how we can distinguish  between causation or correlation as well  as sort of third variables that could be  factors so as an example I guess what  I'm sort of getting to is how could we  know that the most in important factor  that correlates to for example of  mortality is church attendance versus  for example some other factor which  encourages or allows people to go to  church more that might actually be the  causal factor associated with the  reduced mortality rate how is a  scientist can we explore those  possibilities yeah so that's a very good  question and it's what I spend most of  my life and research trying to sort out  and there are really two concerns here  one which you had had mentioned is how  do we know it's not some some third  factor may be married people are more  likely to attend religious services and  are also more likely to live longer how  do we know it's not just marriage or how  do we know it's not just having a higher  income and what we do is we try to  control for those other variables in the  analysis so we controlled for race  education marital status and and  importantly we also controlled for a  baseline health whether they were  depressed at the beginning of the study  whether they had a cardiovascular  condition and controlling for health at  the beginning is also important to  address another potential concern which  is which is that of reverse causation  the worry that it's one finds these  associations simply because it's only  those who are healthy who can actually  attend services and so we try to control  for health it they slide in the study  too and address that the other thing we  do and this is what the real advance was  of this study compared to all prior  studies on this topic is we measured  religious service attendants and health  repeatedly over time and so we can see  which changes and proceeded which others  and so using that sort of data and  methods to address these concerns we  were able to provide evidence that this  relationship was causal even after  controlling for all of these other  variables even looking at the temporal  changes over time the the associations  were quite robust they they held up so  one can't really prove causality with  data of the sort but one can provide  evidence for it and our methods in our  data and suggest the evidence here is  fairly strong when you talk about  adjusting for baseline health I'm  curious as to whether that include sort  of some of the most important factors uh  in terms of lifestyle with regard to  health in other words how was diet  considered in that where diet may be  something that doesn't show in baseline  health conditions but over a period of  observation a  or diet or better diet for example can  start to have health repercussions how  might something like that be a accounted  for in a study like this yeah so we did  control for diet quality as well along  with them smoking social support at  baseline we we made adjustment for all  of those factors with something like  smoking it's quite interesting because  those who smoke regularly are less  likely to attend religious services but  it's also the case that those who attend  religious services and our smoking are  subsequently if they attend religious  service is more likely to stop smoking  so you get effects in both directions  and once again the only way to really  sort through that is by collecting data  repeatedly over time on both religious  service attendants and on something like  smoking or diet or social support so the  way you try to handle these types of  issues is you control for or adjust all  of for all of the behaviors and factors  prior to the religious service  attendants measure but not the ones  subsequent to the religious service  attendants measure that you're looking  at so you can actually get at the effect  of religious service attendants itself  on these other subsequent behaviors and  pathways so once again the temporal  ordering of the data that's collected is  very important when people hear this  conversation I will no doubt get emails  from people saying but wait wait wait is  the argument that belief in God or  religiosity in some way are correlated  or causally related to better health to  be very clear that's not at all what  this study says right this is  specifically related to church  attendance that's right this study was  looking specifically at religious  service attendants and in another study  which will be published in this fall we  also looked at measures of religious or  spiritual identity and religious coping  to deal with stress and private  practices such as prayer and we found  those were much more weakly associated  with  with better health there was still some  Association but it was much weaker than  was the case for religious service  attendant so there seems to be something  very powerful about the communal  religious experience something far more  powerful than than private practice with  regard to beliefs we actually really  don't have very good data hundred  religious beliefs in and and health  there is one intriguing cross-sectional  study so very very weak designed for  trying to establish causality but the  results are intriguing which suggested  that belief in life after death was  associated with better mental health  outcomes but we need much better data  data like the type that was in our study  on religious service attendants to more  accurately assess the and health effects  of different religious belief so I would  say that the the date and the studies on  on that and really haven't good studies  really haven't appeared yes that would  be media I mean I guess certainly we can  all imagine how belief in life after  death could s wage many anxieties in the  real world for people so what I what I  I'm sort of getting to and I came away  with from looking at the details of the  study is that the the data seem to  correlate pretty significantly with what  we see in the studies that we have about  the impact of for example regular  meditation practice as as one a  possibility and that I would imagine  that if we also looked at a population  and said okay what about participation  in regular social coed sports leagues  for example or something like that that  we would see very similar types of  correlations and I'm sort of left with  the question whether what we're really  talking about here is that these types  of engagements and connections to  community aside from scriptural beliefs  are really what seems to be correlated  with and at least in this particular  case you're arguing causally related  with lower mortality and less anxiety  depression etceteras is that a sort of  generally fair assessment it's a very  good question and certainly the social  component of religious service  attendance is and very important and we  we looked at what the different  mechanisms might be for relating  religious service attendants to better  better health and and social support did  explain some of it probably about a  quarter so some but there do seem to the  other pathways as well if you look at  other forms of social participation like  showing up for a a card game or being  involved in a political organization and  those are also associated with with  better health the association's tend to  not be quite as strong as they are with  religious service attendants but but  they're certainly there is well my  speculation though we don't have good  data on this is that social groups which  have not simply a common place to meet  but also a shared set of values and a  common mission and purpose are probably  going to have stronger effects on health  than simply say showing up for a regular  card game so I do think the social  aspect of it provision of social support  is important for other social  organizations and for religious service  attendants but it's it's certainly not  the and the only mechanism so when we  try to look at why this association  appeared to be their social support was  important but there were other things  those who attended religious services I  think because of the social and  behavioral norms present were less  likely to smoke were more likely to quit  smoking if they already smoked and that  goes on to affect health and those who  attended services I think perhaps  because of the message of faith or hope  at these services we're more optimistic  and less likely to subsequently become  depressed and we know that that also  affects health and other mechanisms that  have been proposed in the literature and  blued and religious service is giving  when we're meeting in purpose in life  which has been shown to affect health  and and perhaps by attending religious  services and trying to follow the  practices of these groups one develops  of disciplines health control which is  good for health and other outcomes as  well so I think there are many different  pathways which by which religious  service attendants a fax health and I  think the reason we're finding fairly  large associations is the  cuz there are so many different pathways  it's not just one thing it's not just  social support social supports important  but there are these other mechanisms as  well very very interesting we've been  speaking with professor of Epidemiology  Tyler van der wiel from the Harvard  School of Public Health thanks so much  for talking to us about the study thank  you David
 

